00667572021484375, -0.0313720703125, 0.0302734375, -0.0213470458984375, -0.0028514862060546875, -0.046295166015625, -0.03350830078125, -0.0212860107421875, -0.034698486328125, 0.036102294921875, 0.02862548828125, 0.0196075439453125, 0.006832122802734375, 0.0251922607421875, -0.006336212158203125, 0.01326751708984375, -0.0482177734375, 0.0121612548828125, 0.0012369155883789062, 0.007511138916015625, 0.0266265869140625, -0.0268096923828125, -0.00649261474609375, -0.01464080810546875, -0.017303466796875, -0.0087432861328125, 0.003414154052734375, 0.004772186279296875, 0.0267333984375, 0.0037860870361328125, 0.00806427001953125, -0.0027637481689453125, 0.01528167724609375, -0.0238189697265625, 0.005016326904296875, -0.0010576248168945312, 0.0095367431640625, 0.00916290283203125, -0.0343017578125, -0.011688232421875, -0.00272369384765625, -0.020416259765625, -0.0188751220703125, -0.0015735626220703125, 0.01143646240234375, -0.0318603515625, 0.0098724365234375, -0.0024929046630859375, 0.013702392578125, -0.0029087066650390625, 0.004062652587890625, -0.00417327880859375, 0.006748199462890625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "How level-0 players behave and how they are perceived by higher-level players are central questions in the literature on level-k models of boundedly rational strategic reasoning. We address the research question: How do level-0 actions and beliefs manifest in strategic games, and how do they compare to standard assumptions in level-k models? To investigate this, we use the intra-team communication method developed by Burchardi and Penczynski (2014), which elicits both the actions that teams would choose as level-0 players (level-0 actions) and their beliefs about what a level-0 player would do (level-0 beliefs). This approach allows us to directly observe and compare these two constructs, which are often assumed to coincide but may differ in practice.\n\nWe apply this method to two games: the canonical beauty contest game and a variant of the 11-20 game introduced by Goeree et al. (2018). In the beauty contest game, we replicate Burchardi and Penczynski (2014)'s finding that elicited level-0 beliefs closely match observed level-0 actions, supporting the standard level-k assumption that level-0 players choose actions in line with naive or non-strategic reasoning. In contrast, in the 11-20 game variant\u2014where standard level-k models predict choices that are not observed empirically\u2014behavior deviates from these predictions: elicited level-0 beliefs and observed level-0 actions not only diverge from each other, but both also differ from the standard assumption that level-0 players randomize uniformly or choose dominated actions. This divergence suggests that players may use alternative heuristics or focal points when reasoning about level-0 behavior, providing a complementary explanation for the behavioral patterns documented by Goeree et al. (2018).\n\nOur findings highlight the importance of empirically distinguishing between level-0 actions and beliefs, and suggest that standard assumptions about level-0 behavior may not generalize across games. This has broader implications for the modeling of bounded rationality and strategic reasoning, indicating that more flexible or context-dependent specifications of level-0 behavior may be necessary for accurate predictions."}}